Run this complete example in the TypeScript runner:
```typescript
enum Practice {
  Drawing,
  Singing
}
function pointsFor(minutes: number, kind: Practice): number {
  return Math.floor(minutes / 10);
}
console.log(pointsFor(37, Practice.Drawing));
console.log(pointsFor(9, Practice.Singing));
```
The outputs are 3 and 0. / divides minutes by ten. Math.floor rounds the result down to the nearest integer: 3.7 becomes 3. Only completed ten-minute chunks earn points in this rule.

Continue after that enum and function:
```typescript
class Student {
  points = 0;
  award(minutes: number, kind: Practice): number {
    const earned = pointsFor(minutes, kind);
    this.points += earned;
    return earned;
  }
}
const student = new Student();
console.log(student.award(37, Practice.Drawing));
console.log(student.award(9, Practice.Singing));
console.log(student.points);
```
The outputs are 3, 0, and 3. new Student() creates an object; this refers to that object inside its method. += adds earned to its previous points. return earned returns this session's reward, not the accumulated total.

after:
This example gives one point for each full group of ten minutes.
```typescript
enum Practice { Drawing, Singing }
function pointsFor(minutes: number, kind: Practice): number {
  return Math.floor(minutes / 10);
}
class Student {
  points = 0;
  award(minutes: number, kind: Practice): number {
    const earned = pointsFor(minutes, kind);
    this.points += earned;
    return earned;
  }
}
const student = new Student();
console.log(student.award(37, Practice.Drawing));
```
The output is 3. Dividing 37 by 10 gives 3.7, and Math.floor rounds down to 3.
